    "lawCode": "Amd §64-c, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5427\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in\nrelation to expanding the amount of barrels that a restaurant brewer may\nsell at retail\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill will allow restaurant brewers to sell at retail, in the aggre-\ngate, no more than two thousand barrels of beer per year.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends section 64-a of the alcoholic beverage\ncontrol law, as it relates to expanding the cap on the number of barrels\nof beer that can be sold at retail by restaurant brewers per year. It\neliminates the supplemental license required to sell beer at retail and\nrequires the sale to be through a licensed beer wholesaler.\n\nSection 2 of the bill relates to effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill will expand the amount of barrels that a restaurant brewer\n(brew-pub) may produce to sell at retail or wholesale. The current limit\nis 250 barrels; this legislation would expand that limit to 2,000\nbarrels. The current 250 barrel limit makes it difficult for brew-pubs\nto invest in the infrastructure necessary to grow their retail brand.\n\nBrew-Pubs are a growing business In New York State and certain regions\nare developing a reputation for outstanding products. Some of these\nbusinesses start out small, selling their products in-house.  There is\npotential, however, for these businesses to grow into larger retail and\noff premise production companies. Unfortunately, the 250 barrel limit\nmakes it difficult to justify the infrastructure investments necessary.\nThis limit can reduce their ability to get their product on store\nshelves and into other bars and restaurants.  By expanding the cap on\nretail and wholesale production, restaurant-brewers will still be far\nbelow the micro-brewer production limit of 75,000 barrels, yet they will\nhave an incentive to invest in their brand and product which can help\nthese businesses grow.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.6404-B of 2017-18\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
